<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges Clemenceau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges Clemenceau was a French Radical politician and journalist, known as the Tiger, who defended Dreyfus and served as prime minister in 1906–1909 and 1917–1920. In the last year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he rallied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to victory, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> he pressed for the harsh terms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau.jpg" alt="Georges Clemenceau" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georges_Clemenceau_par_Nadar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georges Benjamin Clemenceau was born on 28 September 1841 in Mouilleron-en-Pareds in the Vendée, a remote, rural and poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. His father, Benjamin Clemenceau, came from a line of physicians but lived off his lands, was a political activist and was arrested in 1851 and 1858. He instilled in his son a love of learning, devotion to radical politics and a hatred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> the young Clemenceau co-founded a weekly, Le Travail, in 1861 and was held for 77 days in Mazas Prison in 1862 after posting notices calling a demonstration. He qualified as a doctor of medicine on 13 May 1865, and in 1869 he married Mary Eliza Plummer in New York City.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the defeat at Sedan in 1870 Clemenceau became mayor of the 18th arrondissement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> seized power in March 1871 he tried without success to find a compromise between its leaders and the government. From 1876 he sat in the Chamber of Deputies for the 18th arrondissement, and his energy and cutting eloquence made him leader of the radicals. After his defeat in 1893 he confined himself for nearly a decade to journalism. On 13 January 1898 he published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a>'s J'Accuse...! on the front page of L'Aurore, which he owned and edited, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair">Dreyfus Affair</a> he published 665 articles defending Dreyfus.</p><p>In 1906 he became Minister of the Interior, reformed the police, supported scientific policing under Alphonse Bertillon and founded the mobile squads nicknamed the Tiger's Brigades. After the war began, his newspaper was censored and, renamed L'Homme enchaîné, criticised the government's lack of transparency while defending the union sacrée. In November 1917, at one of the darkest hours of the war, he became prime minister, governing from the Ministry of War and discouraging internal disagreement. His government passed a general eight-hour day law in April 1919, extended to all classes of workers by later measures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the Paris Peace Conference Clemenceau sought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s frontier and was under strong pressure to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s reparations bill large. On 19 February 1919 the anarchist Émile Cottin fired on his car, and a bullet that stayed in his body for the rest of his life struck him between the ribs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> was signed on 28 June 1919, and he defended it in the French Parliament against critics who thought its compromises inadequate. He resigned as prime minister after the presidential election of 17 January 1920 and took no further part in politics.</p><p>He was a long-time friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a> and persuaded him to complete his donation of the Water Lilies paintings to the French state. He died on 24 November 1929.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georges Clemenceau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mouilleron-en-Pareds, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.68,-0.85&amp;year=1898" aria-label="Mouilleron-en-Pareds on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4530 523 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Georges Clemenceau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4980 793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4980 793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.68,-0.85&amp;year=1898">What was here in 1898? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It learns nothing by discourses; could discourses teach the world, the Sermon on the Mount would have been realized long ago.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the debate on socialism with Jean Jaurès, Chamber of Deputies, 1906 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/In_the_Debate_on_Socialism_with_Jaur%C3%A8s" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You have the magic power of evoking fairy palaces with your wand, while I am as some modest laborer on a cathedral who obscurely carries a stone to the august edifice he shall never see.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the debate on socialism with Jean Jaurès, Chamber of Deputies, 1906 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/In_the_Debate_on_Socialism_with_Jaur%C3%A8s" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You pretend directly to construct the future, while we construct the man who will construct the future, and in so doing we are achieving a phenomenon much greater than your own.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech in the debate on socialism with Jean Jaurès, Chamber of Deputies, 1906 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/In_the_Debate_on_Socialism_with_Jaur%C3%A8s" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
